**LASSO Regression**

$$
OLS\ idea = min_{b,a} \sum (y_i -b_i - \sum X_k , b_k)^2
$$

-   Minimizing subject to constraints

-   This will always be worse than OLS in-sample

$$
Lasso = min_{b,a} \sum (y_i -b_1 - \sum_i^k X_k , b_2)^2 + \lambda \times\sum_n^k |b_3|
$$

-   The researcher sets the lambda regularizer, to penalize coefficients. Lambda = 0 = ols

    -   We choose each coefficient so that the coefficients do not deviate in absolute terms

        -   Lambda value is the magnitude by which we shrink them

    -   Why shrink coefficients close to 0?

        -   if coefficients truly are 0, then any estimated coefficients larger than - would be overfitting

-   L1 penalty = absolute value, L2 = square

-   Looking at how coefficients vary w/ lambda (make coefplot)

    -   create coefficient plot with values of coefficients at different levels

    -   We can also make this plot with the in-sample goodness of fit (and out of sample)

    -   We can also look at the bias

$$
MSE = bias^2 + variance
$$

-   As we shrink parameters, we get more bias form shrinkage, but less (theoretical) overfitting.

-   Because we get less overfitting the variance will go down as bias increases


